      <!-- The main price factors for safe installation -->
      <div class="mt-16 max-w-4xl mx-auto">
        <h3 class="text-3xl font-bold text-neutral-900 text-center">
          The main price factors for safe installation
        </h3>
        
        <div class="mt-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          <ul class="space-y-4 text-left list-disc marker:text-amber-500 pl-5">
            <li><strong>Safe grade and rating:</strong> Higher security safes (for example, Eurograde models) are typically heavier, more complex to position and anchor, and may need more time on-site. If you're aiming to meet an insurer requirement, the safe's rating and "cash rating" expectations can also influence the installation approach.</li>
            <li><strong>Safe weight and dimensions:</strong> Heavier safes may require specialist handling equipment, additional manpower, and more time to manoeuvre safely—especially where there are stairs, tight turns, or limited space to work.</li>
            <li><strong>Access and installation route:</strong> The practical route into the property is often a bigger cost driver than people expect. Narrow doorways, steps, long carry distances from parking, lift restrictions, or multiple flights of stairs can all add complexity.</li>
            <li><strong>Substrate and fixing requirements:</strong> The wall or floor construction matters. Anchoring into solid masonry or concrete is different from dealing with blockwork, suspended timber floors, or modern plasterboard/stud walls. Some sites require heavier-duty fixings, alternative anchor points, or a revised placement plan to achieve the same anti-removal result.</li>
            <li><strong>Making good and finish level:</strong> Some installs are purely functional (secure anchoring with minimal disturbance), while others need a neater finish—such as minor patching, cosmetic cover plates, cupboard adaptations, or discreet finishing around the safe. The more "invisible" you want the final result to be, the more time and materials may be involved.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      
      <!-- What a clear quote should include -->
